\begin{document}
\maketitle

\section{Introduction}
Kombio (also Endangen; ISO 639-3 xbi) is a Torricelli language of East Sepik Province, Papua New Guinea. Wikipedia gives about 3,000 speakers (2003). Joshua Project places the Kombio people in the Dreikikir district across 31 villages, citing Ethnologue 2016. Speakers are shifting to Tok Pisin. The listed dialects are Mwi, Wampukuamp, Yanimoi and Wampurun, and Mwi is the most divergent. Its position within Torricelli is disputed.

\section{Primary Educational Resources}
No primers, textbooks or school programmes for Kombio were found in search results.

\section{Linguistic and Reference Materials}
\begin{itemize}
\item Wikipedia, ``Kombio language''. \url{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kombio_language}
\item Wikipedia, ``Kombio-Arapeshan languages''. \url{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kombio-Arapeshan_languages}
\end{itemize}
No Kombio dictionary or grammar was found.

\section{Religious and Community Resources}
\begin{itemize}
\item Kombio Yanimoi Bible portions (Wycliffe, 2002). \url{https://png.bible/details.php?id=xbi-y}
\item Kombio Wampukuamp Bible portions (Wycliffe/SIL PNG, 2000). \url{https://www.png.bible/details.php?id=xbi-w}
\item Joshua Project profile of the Kombio people. \url{https://joshuaproject.net/people_groups/12760}
\end{itemize}

\section{Digital and Audio Resources}
The Bible portions are available in several digital formats on eBible/png.bible. No audio or video was found.

\section{Curriculum Development Context}
The documentation found is thin: scripture portions in two dialects and encyclopedic descriptions. Dialect differences mean a curriculum would have to choose a standard or develop dialect-specific materials. Fieldwork and community partnership would be needed to supply a dictionary, a grammar and teaching materials.
\end{document}
